## =====================================================================
## USB PicoScope bring-up - 10 kHz square wave on channel A
## =====================================================================
##
## The first thing to run on a rig that has never had a PicoScope on it.
## It takes repeated triggered, segmented captures of a square wave on
## channel A and writes nothing but scope data: no stage moves, no DAQ
## output, nothing else that could be the thing that is broken.
##
##   >>> FEED THE SQUARE WAVE INTO CHANNEL A and leave the generator
##   >>> running. Everything below assumes 10 kHz, 0 to +150 mV.
##
## A square wave is SELF-CHECKING in two independent ways, which is why it
## is a better bring-up signal than a DC level: the two flat levels test
## the range and the volts scaling, and the edge position tests the
## trigger and the timebase. Either one being wrong is obvious in the data
## without a meter and without the GUI.
##
## >>> CLOSE THE PICOSCOPE GUI FIRST. <<<
##
## The PicoSDK hands a unit to ONE process at a time, and PicoScope 7
## holds it for as long as it is connected to the scope. With the GUI open
## pscan reports "no PicoScope was found on USB", which reads exactly like
## a dead cable. Closing the window is enough; there is no service left
## behind. Nothing else about the GUI matters here either - see WHAT THE
## GUI DOES NOT CARRY OVER below.
##
## WHAT THIS IS ACTUALLY TESTING, in order:
##
##   1. pscan can find and open the unit at all (the PicoSDK libraries are
##      installed, the cable is in, the GUI is not holding it);
##   2. the range, the coupling and the trigger in this file reach the
##      hardware - a trigger that never fires is immediately visible as a
##      dropped trace rather than as a hang;
##   3. the ADC counts in the .dat convert to the right volts through
##      scp.vgain - which is the one thing that is silently wrong if the
##      range handling is wrong;
##   4. segmented capture at a realistic depth: 1000 segments per trace,
##      each separately triggered, captured back to back into the unit's
##      own memory and transferred in one go;
##   5. a trace lands per coordinate, in step with the loop.
##
## THIS IS NOT A SMALL RUN - see THE SIZE OF THIS below before starting
## it. If the only question is whether the scope opens at all, cut
## 'segments' to 10 and 'count' to 5 for the first pass and put them back
## afterwards.
##
## WHAT THE GUI DOES NOT CARRY OVER
##
## None of it. The GUI's settings live in the application, not in the
## unit, and pscan opens the hardware from scratch - which is why this
## block spells out every setting that a LeCroy would be asked to report
## about itself. Two consequences worth being explicit about:
##
##   * Rapid capture is how the GUI exposes segmented memory, and pscan
##     does not need to be told to use it: it ALWAYS acquires in rapid
##     block mode (MemorySegments / SetNoOfCaptures / GetValuesBulk), even
##     for a single segment. The DEPTH comes from 'segments' below, and
##     nowhere else.
##   * 5 us/div is a GUI screen setting with no equivalent here. A record
##     is a sample rate and a number of samples, so the length is written
##     as 'n_samples' - which at 20 MS/s works out to the same 50 us.
##
## ONE WORD, THREE NAMES. The same number is called 'segments' by the
## LeCroy and by pscan's own 'action scope' block, 'rapid captures' by the
## PicoScope GUI, and 'captures' by the PicoSDK (SetNoOfCaptures). This
## block accepts 'segments' or 'captures' for it; they are one key. The
## driver and the log say "capture(s)" because that is the SDK's word, so
## do not read that line as reporting something other than what is
## written here.
##
## HOW TO READ THIS FILE
##
## The scope block sits INSIDE the count block, and that is what says it
## runs once per repeat:
##
##   for each of 100 repeats            <- action count
##       capture one trace             <- action picoscope
##         ... of 1000 segments        <- 'segments', inside the block
##
## There is no axis in this file on purpose: the only loop is 'count', so
## nothing physical moves and a bad trace cannot be blamed on a stage. See
## examples/nested_loops.con for the general rules; the two that matter
## here are that only a loop (an axis block, or 'count') may contain other
## blocks, and that every recording block must sit at the same depth.
##
## Note that 'count' and 'segments' are NOT interchangeable, even though
## both multiply the number of records. 1000 segments are one armed
## acquisition: the unit triggers, stores and rearms by itself, with no
## software in between, and the whole 2 MB comes back in a single
## transfer. 100 counts are 100 separate acquisitions, each going through
## pscan's whole per-coordinate path. The split below tests both.
##
## Run it in this order:
##
##   pscan --dry-run examples/bringup_picoscope.con
##   pscan --timer   examples/bringup_picoscope.con
##
## --dry-run opens nothing and prints the nest, the grid and the output
## files. Do it first; it catches a typo in this file before the hardware
## is touched. It also prints the line
##
##   This config NESTS its blocks, so the file decides the order
##
## which is how you confirm the hierarchy parsed as written rather than
## being read by the flat positional rule. What --dry-run echoes back is
## what is WRITTEN here - "+/-0.15V, 1000 samples @ 20 MS/s" - because it
## opens nothing and so cannot know what the unit will round those to.
## The armed line in a real run is the one that reports what took.
##
## WHAT THE OPENING LOG SHOULD SAY. There is no 'pydaq list' for a
## PicoScope - the first run IS the detection - so read these two lines
## before trusting anything after them:
##
##   -> PicoScope 5244D (serial GQ123/456), ps5000a driver 2.1.x, via
##      /opt/picoscope/lib/libps5000a.so.2
##   -> PicoScope armed: A at +/-0.2V, 1000 samples @ 20.83 MS/s
##      (asked 20 MS/s), 1000 capture(s)
##
## The model and serial are read off the unit, not out of this file, so
## they confirm WHICH box answered. The second line is the configuration
## that actually took, and two things in it are expected to differ from
## what is written below:
##
##   +/-0.2V, not 0.15      Pico ranges are a fixed ladder and symmetric
##                          about ground: 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500 mV and
##                          up. There is no 150 mV range, so pscan takes
##                          the narrowest one that CONTAINS 150 mV. It
##                          never widens silently past that - a 'v_max' no
##                          range can hold is refused with the numbers.
##   20.83 MS/s, not 20     the timebase ladder is discrete too (on a
##                          5000A at 8 bits the rungs near here are 48 ns
##                          and 56 ns, so 50 ns does not exist). pscan
##                          takes the nearest rung that is NOT SLOWER than
##                          asked, so a record is never quietly stretched.
##                          The achieved interval is what goes in scp.hint
##                          and is what the check below uses. It also
##                          makes the record 48 us rather than 50.
##
## If instead the achieved rate comes back far below 20 MS/s, the unit has
## no timebase that fast for this many samples and channels, and the log
## says so in a WARNING of its own.
##
## IF IT DOES NOT OPEN, the message says which of three things happened:
##
##   "no PicoSDK library could be loaded"  the driver packages are not
##       installed. Run 'bash install.sh' with the scope plugged in; see
##       README under OSCILLOSCOPES. Nothing to do with the cable.
##   "no PicoScope was found on USB"  the libraries are there and no unit
##       answered. THE GUI BEING OPEN IS THE USUAL CAUSE - check that
##       first, then the cable and the light. A 2000/3000 series unit
##       WITHOUT an 'A' in the model number uses a different PicoSDK
##       library and is not driven by pscan at all.
##   "N PicoScope units are attached"  more than one is plugged in, and
##       pscan refuses to guess which one you meant. It prints the exact
##       'series' and 'serial' lines to paste into the block below.
##
## IF IT OPENS AND THEN REFUSES TO ARM, the likely message is
##
##   "this unit holds N sample(s) per segment when its memory is split
##    1000 ways ... but the block asks for 1000"
##
## which is capture memory, not a fault. 1000 segments of 1000 samples
## needs ONE MEGASAMPLE of buffer. Anything from a 3000A upwards has far
## more than that; a small 2000A may not, and the message gives the real
## number so 'segments' can be set to what the unit will actually hold.
## pscan asks the unit before arming rather than shortening the record
## behind your back.
##
## THE SIZE OF THIS
##
##   per trace     1000 segments x 1000 samples x int16 = 2 MB,
##                 and 1000 triggers
##   whole run     100 traces -> 200 MB in bringup_picoscope_pico0.dat
##
## 1000 triggers at 10 kHz is at best 100 ms of wall clock, and the 2 MB
## then has to cross USB, so budget somewhere around 0.2 s per trace and
## tens of seconds for the run. That is a guess; --timer is what replaces
## it with the real number, and getting that number for a full-depth
## segmented capture is half the reason to run this at all. Worth writing
## the result into this header once you have it, as the baseline to
## compare against if a real scan ever feels slow.
##
## EXPECTED OUTPUT
##
##   bringup_picoscope_pico0.dat   100 x 1000 x 1000 x int16 = 200000000
##                                 bytes
##   bringup_picoscope_pico0.m     the geometry and the volts scaling
##
## There is no _trigtime.dat. A PicoScope writes no trigger-time sidecar
## even when it is triggered, which is the one format difference from a
## LeCroy Sequence run; see README under OSCILLOSCOPES. It matters here:
## WITHOUT IT THERE IS NO RECORD OF WHEN EACH SEGMENT AROSE, so the 10 kHz
## cannot be recovered from the segment spacing. See the note under the
## frequency check below.
##
## The .m should say points_per_trace=1000, n_traces=100000,
## n_channels=1, multitraces=1000 and n_averages=[1]. n_traces counts
## SEGMENTS, not coordinates: 100 repeats x 1000 segments x 1 channel.
##
## CHECK THE DATA WITHOUT LEAVING THE SHELL. Reads only the first few
## segments, so it does not pull 200 MB through memory:
##
##   python3 -c "
##   import re, numpy as np
##   m = open('bringup_picoscope_pico0.m').read()
##   num = lambda k: float(re.search(r'scp\.'+k+r'=\[?([^,;\]]+)', m).group(1))
##   g, o, dt = num('vgain'), num('voff'), num('hint')
##   n, pre = 1000, 100
##   d = np.fromfile('bringup_picoscope_pico0.dat', dtype=np.int16, count=4*n)
##   d = g * d.reshape(-1, n).astype(float) - o
##   print(f'{dt*1e9:.2f} ns/sample, record {n*dt*1e6:.1f} us')
##   for i, s in enumerate(d):
##       lo, hi = s[:pre-10].mean(), s[pre+10:].mean()
##       mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)
##       x = np.flatnonzero((s[:-1] < mid) & (s[1:] >= mid))
##       at = x[0] if len(x) else -1
##       print(f'  seg {i}  before {lo*1e3:+7.2f} mV  after {hi*1e3:+7.2f} mV'
##             f'  step {(hi-lo)*1e3:6.2f} mV  edge at sample {at} (want {pre})')
##   "
##
## At 20.83 MS/s a 1000-sample record is 48 us, which is UNDER half the
## 100 us period - so each segment holds the rising edge and the high
## level after it, and nothing else. That is the same window the GUI shows
## at 5 us/div, and it is enough to check the levels, the trigger and the
## edge placement, but NOT the frequency: one edge cannot give a period.
## To check the 10 kHz itself, run once with 'segments 10' and
## 'n_samples 4000' - 192 us is two full periods, three edges - and then
## put this file back.
##
## WHAT THE ANSWERS MEAN
##
##   before ~0, after ~150 mV, edge     everything works. Go and write the
##     at sample 100 every time         real config.
##   edge at sample 0 or missing        'pretrigger' did not take, or the
##                                      trigger fired on the wrong thing.
##   both levels near 0                 either the cable is not in A, or
##                                      'coupling' is ac - see the note on
##                                      that line.
##   levels a constant FACTOR out       a range problem: every segment 2x
##     (all 2x, all 0.5x)               or 0.5x is the classic symptom of
##                                      the wrong range index.
##   levels flat-topped at +/-200 mV    the input clipped. The log warns
##                                      about this by itself; widen
##                                      'v_max'.
##   first segments fine, later ones    look at the END of the file rather
##     flat                             than the start - a padded trace is
##                                      flat, and so is a dead cable.
##                                      Check for a _dropped.txt first.
##   "did not finish a capture within"  the trigger stopped firing part
##                                      way through the 1000. With the
##                                      generator running, suspect
##                                      'timeout' before suspecting the
##                                      scope - 1000 segments is a long
##                                      wait by design.
##
## At +/-200 mV on an 8-bit unit one count is 1.56 mV, so expect the flat
## levels to sit within a couple of mV of nominal and no closer. That is
## resolution, not a fault - and it is why a real measurement uses the
## narrowest range the signal fits inside. A 5000A can do better here
## without changing the range at all; see the 'resolution' note below.
##
## Reload in MATLAB exactly as a LeCroy Sequence run: same .dat layout,
## same .m fields, segments in the same order. That is the point of the
## format being shared.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## 100 REPEATS, so this is a scan and not a single shot.
##
## 'count' is the loop here only because nothing physical should move
## during a scope bring-up. A hundred passes over the same signal answer
## the question one acquisition cannot: is it REPEATABLE, or did one of
## them come back short, padded, or at a different amplitude. Compare the
## passes, do not average them - which is exactly why this is 'count 100'
## and not 'averages 100' inside the block below.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
action count
count 100

    ## -----------------------------------------------------------------
    ## ONE TRACE OF 1000 SEGMENTS PER REPEAT.
    ##
    ## Every setting is here because a USB scope has no front panel to
    ## inherit one from - that is the difference from 'action scope', and
    ## it is why nothing set in the GUI matters.
    ##
    ## 'series'/'serial' are commented out: with one unit attached pscan
    ## finds it and names it in the log. Uncomment and paste in what the
    ## error prints if you ever have two plugged in.
    ##
    ## 'v_max 0.15' is the FULL SCALE in volts, so this asks for a range
    ## that reaches 150 mV and gets +/-200 mV, the narrowest rung that
    ## contains it. Write the signal's real amplitude here and let pscan
    ## pick; do not round up to a range yourself.
    ##
    ## 'coupling dc' MATTERS HERE and is the classic bring-up trap. This
    ## square wave swings 0 to +150 mV, so its average is +75 mV: AC
    ## coupling would subtract exactly that, shifting the trace to
    ## +/-75 mV about ground - which also puts it under the 75 mV trigger
    ## threshold below, so the capture would then time out rather than
    ## read wrong. One wrong word, two symptoms.
    ##
    ## 'sample_rate 20e6' is a REQUEST. See the log note above: the unit
    ## rounds it up to the nearest timebase it has, and scp.hint in the .m
    ## is the interval actually used.
    ##
    ## 'n_samples 1000' is the record LENGTH, which replaces 5 us/div:
    ## 1000 samples at 20 MS/s is 50 us, the same window as 5 us/div
    ## across ten divisions. It is under half a period of a 10 kHz wave,
    ## so a segment holds one rising edge and the high level after it -
    ## see the note on the frequency check above.
    ##
    ## 'pretrigger 10' keeps the first 10% of the record (100 samples,
    ## about 5 us) from BEFORE the trigger, so the low level and the
    ## rising edge that started the capture are both in the data. With 0
    ## the record starts at the edge and the first sample is already
    ## climbing - and there would be nothing to check the edge placement
    ## against.
    ##
    ## 'trigger A 0.075 rising' triggers on the signal itself. The level
    ## is in VOLTS, absolute, not a percentage and not ADC counts, and
    ## 75 mV is mid-swing of a 0..150 mV wave. Mid-swing on purpose: a
    ## threshold near either flat level will double-trigger on overshoot
    ## or ringing, and one outside the swing never fires at all. It must
    ## also lie inside the range the source channel can see, which pscan
    ## checks before arming.
    ##
    ## 'segments 1000' is the rapid capture depth - the GUI's Rapid
    ## capture count, the PicoSDK's SetNoOfCaptures, and the same key as
    ## 'captures'. A thousand separately triggered records are stored in
    ## the unit's own memory and transferred over USB in one go, which is
    ## the whole reason the mode exists: no software round trip between
    ## segments. They are written as a thousand consecutive rows of the
    ## .dat, exactly as LeCroy Sequence mode writes them, and
    ## scp.multitraces records the thousand.
    ##
    ## Each segment still needs its own trigger. At 10 kHz the wave offers
    ## a rising edge every 100 us while each record is 48 us long, so the
    ## unit has roughly half a period to rearm in; whether it catches
    ## every edge or every other one is a property of the model, not a
    ## fault, and it sets whether a trace takes 100 ms or 200 ms. 'timeout'
    ## below has to cover whichever it turns out to be.
    ##
    ## 'averages 1' keeps the segments separate. 'averages N' would mean
    ## N segments into ONE written row, which is faster than N traces
    ## because neither the stage nor the software ever sees them - but it
    ## cannot be undone later, so it has no place in a bring-up. Note that
    ## averages MULTIPLIES segments when the memory is split, so raising
    ## it here is not free.
    ##
    ## 'timeout 10.0' is the wait for the WHOLE acquisition, all thousand
    ## segments, not one - which is why it is 10 s here and not the 5 that
    ## suits a single capture. A thousand triggers at 10 kHz is around
    ## 0.1-0.2 s when the generator is running, so 10 s is roughly fifty
    ## times the expected wait: long enough that only a real failure
    ## reaches it, short enough that a dead generator does not stall the
    ## run for a minute. A capture that does not finish in time is a
    ## DROPPED trace, not a hang - see the bottom of this file.
    ##
    ## 'resolution 12' is commented out because it only exists on the
    ## 5000A series, which has a switchable ADC; on anything else the line
    ## is ignored with a warning. If this is a 5000A, uncomment it once
    ## the run above is correct: 12 bits at 20 MS/s is well within what
    ## the unit will do on one channel, and it turns 1.56 mV per count
    ## into 0.1 mV per count on the same +/-200 mV range. Change one thing
    ## at a time, though - not during first contact.
    ## -----------------------------------------------------------------
    action picoscope
    ## series ps5000a
    ## serial GQ123/456
    ## resolution 12
    channels A
    v_max 0.15
    coupling dc
    sample_rate 20e6
    n_samples 1000
    pretrigger 10
    trigger A 0.075 rising
    segments 1000
    averages 1
    timeout 10.0
    end

end

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## A DROPPED TRACE IS NOT A HANG
##
## A capture that does not complete does not stop the run: after 'timeout'
## seconds the whole trace is padded, its index is recorded in
## bringup_picoscope_dropped.txt, and the scan carries on. Ten in a row
## aborts it. If that file exists after a run, read it before analysing
## anything - a padded trace looks like a flat line, which is also what a
## dead cable looks like. At this depth that matters more than usual: one
## missed trigger anywhere in a thousand pads the entire 2 MB trace, not
## just the segment that missed.
##
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## WHERE TO GO FROM HERE
##
## ADD AN AXIS. Replace the 'count' block with the real scan axis and the
## scope block does not change at all; that is the point of the nesting:
##
##   action apt_stage            ## or d2a, or pi_stage
##   axis 0
##   ...
##       action picoscope
##       ...
##       end
##   end
##
## Mind the arithmetic when you do: 1000 segments is 2 MB per coordinate,
## so a 100 x 100 raster of this block is 20 GB. Segment depth is the
## number to think hardest about in a real config, and it is the reason
## this file is worth running before one gets written.
##
## Keep 'count' as well if each point should be repeated - but mind WHERE
## it goes. Inside the axis it repeats a point, outside it repeats the
## whole scan; examples/nested_loops.con works that difference through.
##
## ADD THE SECOND CHANNEL. Per-channel keys take one value per channel,
## in the order 'channels' lists them:
##
##   channels A, B
##   v_max 0.15, 5.0           ## signal tight, monitor wide
##   coupling dc, dc
##   trigger A 0.075 rising    ## still on A; B just rides along
##
## Both channels are transferred in full for every segment, so this
## doubles both the file and the USB cost per trace - 4 MB a trace here.
## It also halves the capture memory available per segment, which at this
## depth is the limit you will meet first.
